course: dictionary
lesson: 2868
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Domino» can name a small tile, a game played with tiles, or a chain of events. Look at the words around it to find which meaning the writer means.
explain: A domino is a small flat rectangular tile with spots on each half, from 0 to 6. When you play dominoes, you match the spots on one tile with the spots on the next.
explain: «History is full of chains like a line of dominoes: push the first one, and the last one falls far away, years or even centuries later.» The writer is not talking about a game. Words like chains, push and falls show that one thing sets off the next, so the word means a chain of events.
fact: A domino is a small flat rectangular tile with spots (0 to 6) on each half.
fact: Dominoes is the game played with these tiles.
fact: The domino effect is when one event causes a chain of other events.
fact: «Fall like dominoes» means many things fall one after another.
fact: In maths, dominoes can be used for counting, adding and making pairs.
exercise: Ben said, «Let's play dominoes after tea.» What does dominoes mean here? | *The game played with small flat tiles | A type of cake | A long walk in the park | A pile of old books
exercise: Ella said, «One broken plate set off a domino effect in the cupboard.» What does domino effect mean? | A spotted tile with the number six | Two tiles placed side by side | Playing a game with friends | *One event causes a chain of other events
exercise: What does «fall like dominoes» mean? | *Many things fall one after another, each knocking down the next | Things are made of wood | Things are stacked up very high | Things fall one at a time, with no reason
test: A domino has spots from 0 to 6 on each half. What is the most spots one half can have? | 5 | *6 | 10 | 7
test: In «History is full of chains like a line of dominoes», what is the writer comparing to a line of dominoes? | A tall building | *A chain of events, where one thing sets off the next | A game of cards | A quiet holiday
exam: Which sentence uses domino to mean the game? | «One domino fell over.» | «The domino effect changed the whole town.» | *«We played dominoes after supper.»
